<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benedicto Kiwanuka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benedicto Kiwanuka was a Ugandan lawyer and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> who became Uganda's first chief minister in 1961 and its first prime minister under internal self-government in 1962, before losing power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a> in the elections that preceded independence. Appointed chief justice by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a> in 1971, he defied the regime and in 1972 was seized from his court and murdered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benedicto-kiwanuka.jpg" alt="Benedicto Kiwanuka" width="164" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abbie Rowe, 1905-1967, White house photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benedicto_Kiwanuka,_1961_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
